{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"a9c27cbc-865b-47d7-84b9-c898a8070d7b","title":"Projectile motion: horizontal and vertical velocity"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9c27cbc-865b-47d7-84b9-c898a8070d7b","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Ignoring air resistance, a projectile follows a curved path. We split its velocity into independent horizontal and vertical components.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Projectile velocity"},{"op":"equation","id":"initial","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v_{x0}=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s},\\quad v_{y0}=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"},{"op":"figure","id":"flight","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-1,22],"y":[-1,8],"axes":"edge","equal_scale":true,"x_label":"x (m)","y_label":"y (m)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"path","y":"x-x^2/20","domain":[0,20]},{"kind":"point","id":"launch","at":{"on":"path","x":0},"label":"launch"},{"kind":"point","id":"rising","at":{"on":"path","x":4}},{"kind":"point","id":"top","at":{"on":"path","x":10}},{"kind":"point","id":"falling","at":{"on":"path","x":16}}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9c27cbc-865b-47d7-84b9-c898a8070d7b","step":{"id":"s2","script":"There is no horizontal acceleration. Equal horizontal arrows show that horizontal velocity stays ten meters per second throughout the flight.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"horizontal","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rising"},"to":[7,3.2],"arrow":true,"label":"10 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"top"},"to":[13,5],"arrow":true,"label":"10 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"falling"},"to":[19,3.2],"arrow":true,"label":"10 m/s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"vxlaw","anchor":{"el":"initial","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a_x=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_x=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9c27cbc-865b-47d7-84b9-c898a8070d7b","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Gravity changes only vertical velocity. Taking upward as positive and gravity as ten meters per second squared, vertical velocity decreases steadily.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"vertical","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"rising"},"to":[4,5],"arrow":true,"label":"+6 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"falling"},"to":[16,1.4],"arrow":true,"label":"−6 m/s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"vylaw","anchor":{"el":"vxlaw","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a_y=-10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2},\\qquad v_y=10-10t\\ \\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9c27cbc-865b-47d7-84b9-c898a8070d7b","step":{"id":"s4","script":"At the highest point, vertical velocity is zero, but horizontal velocity remains. The projectile is still moving, and gravity still accelerates it downward.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"apexnote","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[10,6.4],"text":"vᵧ = 0"},{"kind":"segment","from":[10,4.5],"to":[10,2.5],"arrow":true,"label":"gravity"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"apexresult","anchor":{"el":"vylaw","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"t=1\\,\\mathrm{s}:\\quad v_y=0,\\quad |\\mathbf v|=10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At the highest point, which velocity component is zero, and is acceleration zero?","expected_gist":"Vertical velocity is zero; acceleration remains downward."}}}
